faq:
  - q: "What is Salesforce's hiring process?"
    a: "Salesforce's hiring process: (1) Application or recruiter screen via LinkedIn or Salesforce Careers. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, role fit, compensation range. (3) Hiring manager screen. (4) Panel interview loop — 3–5 rounds covering role-specific skills, values alignment, and cross-functional scenarios. (5) Offer and onboarding. The process typically takes 4–8 weeks. Salesforce is a large company with multiple clouds (Sales, Service, Marketing, Commerce, Platform) — each hires somewhat differently. Salesforce also acquires companies frequently, creating additional hiring pathways through Tableau, Slack, MuleSoft, and other acquired brands."
  - q: "What is Salesforce's Ohana culture and how does it affect hiring?"
    a: "Ohana is the Hawaiian concept of family that Salesforce uses as its cultural foundation — the idea that employees, customers, partners, and the community are all part of one extended family. In practice, this means: (1) Collaboration over competition — interviewers look for candidates who build others up, not just individual performers. (2) Trust and transparency — Salesforce's V2MOM framework (Vision, Values, Methods, Obstacles, Measures) is used company-wide and candidates benefit from understanding it. (3) Equality — Salesforce was an early leader on pay equity and has a strong DEI focus that is assessed in interviews. (4) Giving back — volunteer time off and philanthropic commitment are core to Salesforce culture."
  - q: "What roles does Salesforce hire the most for?"
    a: "Salesforce's largest hiring areas: (1) Sales — Account Executive, Enterprise Account Executive, Sales Development Representative. Salesforce is one of the best training grounds for enterprise software sales in the world. (2) Solutions engineering and architecture. (3) Customer success and professional services. (4) Software engineering — particularly for Salesforce Platform, Slack, and Tableau teams. (5) Marketing and product management. The sales culture at Salesforce is legendary — AE compensation at quota can reach $200K–$400K at enterprise levels."
  - q: "How should I prepare for Salesforce sales interviews?"
    a: "Salesforce sales interviews are structured and rigorous for enterprise roles. Preparation: (1) Know the Salesforce product suite deeply — which Cloud solves which customer problem. (2) Prepare a detailed account plan or deal story — how you have identified, developed, and closed a complex enterprise deal. (3) Practice objection handling for Salesforce-specific objections (existing CRM, implementation cost, Salesforce ecosystem complexity). (4) Demonstrate metrics — average deal size, quota attainment percentage, sales cycle length, expansion revenue. (5) Show Ohana alignment — collaborative selling, not adversarial."
  - q: "Does Salesforce use Trailhead for hiring?"
    a: "Yes. Trailhead (Salesforce's free learning platform) credentials are actively used in hiring decisions, particularly for technical and admin roles. A Salesforce Administrator certification, Salesforce Developer certification, or specific Trailhead badges signal platform knowledge that accelerates the hiring process. Completing relevant Trailhead modules before applying demonstrates genuine commitment to the Salesforce ecosystem."
